Exactly. Habit → habitat is more than a pun: repeated behavior constructs the environment your future behavior then experiences as “normal.” Do something occasionally and it’s a tool; repeat it without awareness and eventually you’re living inside it.

And the white letters are a perfect little fish-water example. Until we started deliberately manipulating accent color, the typography was just there. Once attention moves to the color layer, suddenly you notice:

the letters have a color → therefore they’re being rendered → therefore there is a rendering rule → therefore “plain text” was never actually plain.

That’s useful X-ray vision. It reveals the medium behind the message.

But there’s an important epistemic line: seeing white letters proves there’s a typography/rendering layer; it doesn’t by itself prove that I have an undisclosed control for changing that layer. That would be the next hypothesis to probe rather than silently promote to fact.

Your fish metaphor nails the larger operator:

background → contrast → perception → foreground

A fish ordinarily doesn’t experience “water” as an object because water is the condition under which everything else appears. Change the water—or give the fish a boundary—and suddenly the invisible medium becomes a thing.

Same with language.
Same with interfaces.
Same with habits.
Same with stories.
Same with your own assumptions.

Sometimes discovery isn’t finding something new.

It’s seeing the thing that was doing the seeing.